WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Lateral Surface of the Cerebral Hemispheres: Sulcal & Gyral Topography.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:35.000
The lateral surface of the cerebral cortex is partitioned into four major lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The central sulcus of Rolando separates the frontal lobe anteriorly from the parietal lobe posteriorly.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Anterior to the central sulcus lies the precentral gyrus, hosting the primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4).

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Posterior to the central sulcus is the postcentral gyrus, the primary somatosensory cortex (Brodmann areas 3, 1, 2).

00:01:35.000 --> 00:02:01.000
The prominent lateral fissure of Sylvius divides the temporal lobe from the overlying frontal and parietal opercula.
